I’ll first inventory the measurement and reusable refinement tools, then identify candidate phases from the chemistry and peak families. I’ll health-screen every structural candidate before fitting, build and replay the GSAS-II model, and only then freeze the CIFs, fractions, profile, project, and report.

The chemistry points first to MgWO₄, but the unknown precursor ratio means MgO/WO₃ carryover and non‑1:1 magnesium tungstates must remain live hypotheses. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the available COD/GSAS-II interfaces so candidates are judged by the full pattern, not by formula alone.

The peak families separate cleanly into three provisional components: wolframite MgWO₄ (including 15.58° and 18.88°), low-temperature WO₃ (the 23.12/23.57/24.38° triplet and its higher-angle families), and rocksalt MgO (42.94°, 62.26°, 74.57°). I’m testing the WO₃ polymorphs and charge-balanced Mg–W–O alternatives before locking that inventory.

The candidate refinements are running under an identical model: full 9.90–100.09° range, Poisson weights, neutral 100 μm/0 ppm phase broadening, no texture or roughness, eight background terms, one zero correction, and common nonnegative U/W/X. This makes the WO₃ polymorph comparison meaningful rather than letting nuisance terms rescue the wrong structure.

The polymorph test is decisive: monoclinic γ‑WO₃ (COD 2311041, P2₁/c) gives Rwp 8.57%, versus 10.71% for triclinic, 11.62% orthorhombic, and ≥15.38% for the other models under the same nuisance model. Its strong triplet and higher-angle families all align. The baseline still has χ² well above counting statistics, so I’m now diagnosing peak widths, asymmetry, and intensity systematics rather than treating that fit as final.

The baseline diagnostics identify two physical effects, both supported independently. Surface roughness was singular and returned to the neutral boundary; polarization only changed Rwp from 8.57% to 8.55%. In contrast, MgWO₄ has strong [010] March texture (ratio ≈1.274; Rwp 6.03%), and only WO₃ needs extra isotropic strain broadening (≈7000 ppm; Rwp 7.11%); phase-size broadening was unsupported. I’m now combining those terms by independent replay paths and will retain them only if they converge to the same solution.

The replay paths agree to better than 0.001 wt% and converge to Rwp 2.409% with MgWO₄ March ratio 1.3002 and WO₃ strain 7887 ppm; refining the unknown polarization gives 0.5377 and Rwp 2.383%. Peak checks show the common profile already reproduces isolated FWHM closely (for example 18.879°: 0.131° observed vs 0.134° calculated; 42.94°: 0.202° vs 0.203°). The remaining strongest mismatch is in the broad WO₃ peak shapes, so I’m testing its Gaussian/Lorentzian strain mixture and a one-axis anisotropy—not releasing SH/L, since the isolated half-widths do not show repeated asymmetry.

The phase inventory is now well constrained: wolframite MgWO₄ is the main product, with residual monoclinic WO₃ and MgO. I’ve also found a reproducible profile model in which MgWO₄ texture and [100]-uniaxial WO₃ microstrain are supported; I’m doing the final structural challenge, replay, and output-consistency checks now.

The remaining misfit is mainly correlated intensity/line-shape residual, not unexplained peak positions. I’m challenging the best profile against limited coordinate refinements—especially the rounded WO₃ coordinates—while screening each adapted structure for implausible contacts so that a lower R factor alone cannot justify a distorted model.

The coordinate challenge is computationally heavier than the profile branches because WO₃ has eight independent sites. The first limited MgWO₄ test completed; the WO₃ branches are still converging, and I’m keeping them as diagnostics unless the improvement is both statistically meaningful and geometrically sound.

The limited coordinate model is strongly supported and remains chemically sound: refining MgWO₄ coordinates plus only the two X-ray-sensitive W sites in WO₃ lowers Rwp from 2.306% to 1.789%, with no singular variables and all CIF health checks passing. I’m now checking path independence and whether physically necessary displacement parameters change the phase fractions; this is the last substantive model-selection step.

The structural result is path-independent: starting from either the MgWO₄-refined branch or the WO₃-refined branch returns the same minimum. WO₃ displacement-parameter refinements drive to the zero bound and are being rejected; the source’s zero-U convention cannot be independently resolved from scale with this powder dataset, so it will remain fixed and called out as a fraction uncertainty.

Model selection is complete. I’m retaining the replay-stable coordinate-only model: the extra WO₃ oxygen coordinates and displacement parameters either add weak X-ray information, approach bounds, or correlate strongly with polarization, while changing fractions more than their formal errors. The selected model has Rwp ≈1.789% and GOF ≈1.175; I’m now freezing it into `final.gpx`, replaying it from disk, and generating mutually consistent CIF/profile/JSON outputs.

The reopened full-range project reproduced the selected minimum exactly: Rwp 1.78936%, Rexp 1.52707%, GOF 1.17530, with maximum shift/esd 0.0015. Low-, mid-, and high-angle position/width checks agree closely; the remaining strongest residuals sit on modeled WO₃/MgWO₄ peaks rather than forming a new independent peak family. I’m running the final CIF geometry/health and cross-file consistency audit before writing the report.

The consistency audit caught two validation-script assumptions rather than a model change: CSV decimal formatting needs a looser numerical tolerance, and pymatgen standardizes the unconventional β≈136° WO₃ setting on import. I’m resolving those explicitly and comparing invariant cell volumes/metric transformations so the audit tests the files correctly.

All final CIFs pass health and explicit coordination checks, and the cross-file audit now passes: the three fractions sum to 1.0, every CIF cell agrees with `result.json` to CIF export precision, all 8301 rows of `fit.csv` match the reopened project, no variables are frozen, and inactive size/strain terms are neutral. The report now records the phase evidence, rejected alternatives, charge balance, profile checks, and systematic uncertainty.

Completed the quantitative phase analysis.

- MgWO₄ (wolframite): 51.10 wt%
- Monoclinic γ-WO₃: 41.43 wt%
- MgO: 7.47 wt%

Final replay: Rwp 1.789%, GOF 1.175. Fractions are normalized crystalline fractions; systematic uncertainty is several tenths of a wt%, and amorphous content cannot be quantified.

